WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.989
A terminal here, a file browser there,
notes somewhere else.

00:00:04.409 --> 00:00:07.609
Working on your servers usually means
juggling all three.

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:11.278
WebSSH puts them in one workspace, in
your browser,

00:00:11.278 --> 00:00:13.381
running on a machine you control.

00:00:14.551 --> 00:00:15.800
This is the overview.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:19.445
At a glance you see which hosts are
ready, what's open,

00:00:19.445 --> 00:00:21.731
and how your last automation runs went.

00:00:22.151 --> 00:00:24.447
The map shows how you reach each
machine,

00:00:24.447 --> 00:00:26.575
including the ones behind a jump host.

00:00:26.995 --> 00:00:30.451
If something needs a look, like a host
key you haven't verified,

00:00:30.451 --> 00:00:31.369
it shows up here.

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:33.155
And the layout is yours:

00:00:33.415 --> 00:00:36.273
move panels, resize them, hide what you
don't use.

00:00:36.693 --> 00:00:38.680
And there are eleven themes to choose
from.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.113
Adding hosts is quick.

00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:45.975
Name a network range, and WebSSH looks
for SSH servers in it,

00:00:45.975 --> 00:00:47.286
only when you ask.

00:00:47.706 --> 00:00:49.933
Pick one, and the form is already filled
in.

00:00:50.353 --> 00:00:53.835
Choose a saved key, route through a jump
host, and you're connected.

00:00:55.005 --> 00:00:57.962
Then there's the terminal, where you'll
spend most of your time.

00:00:58.382 --> 00:00:59.989
Every connection gets a tab.

00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:03.115
Put sessions side by side, and keep
notes,

00:01:03.115 --> 00:01:06.659
files and diagnostics next to the shell
they belong to.

00:01:07.079 --> 00:01:08.567
And with tmux on the server,

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:11.277
a session keeps running when you close
the browser.

00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:14.350
Files sit right next to the terminal.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.336
Two panes, each with its own server and
path.

00:01:17.756 --> 00:01:19.293
Open a file, change a line,

00:01:19.293 --> 00:01:22.254
and review the diff before anything is
written back.

00:01:22.674 --> 00:01:24.465
Bigger transfers go into a queue,

00:01:24.465 --> 00:01:27.069
and can run straight from one server to
another.

00:01:28.239 --> 00:01:30.869
For the things you do again and again,
build a workflow.

00:01:31.289 --> 00:01:34.274
A few commands, in order, with the hosts
they should run on.

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.681
Run it now, or put it on a schedule.

00:01:37.101 --> 00:01:40.472
Afterwards you see the result per host,
and where the hosts differ.

00:01:41.642 --> 00:01:43.697
All of it sits behind your own sign-in.

00:01:44.117 --> 00:01:47.538
Passkeys, an authenticator app, or your
identity provider.

00:01:47.958 --> 00:01:50.440
Host keys are checked before you
connect,

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.737
private keys are stored encrypted, and
every account has its own hosts,

00:01:54.737 --> 00:01:55.827
keys and settings.

00:01:56.997 --> 00:01:57.939
Want to try it?

00:01:58.359 --> 00:02:03.223
Download one Compose file, run one
command, and it's up on port 5000.

00:02:03.643 --> 00:02:07.427
For anything facing the internet, follow
the production guide first.

00:02:07.847 --> 00:02:09.684
WebSSH is open source.

00:02:10.104 --> 00:02:12.991
You'll find everything at getwebssh.com.
